---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "gainful protein powder",
      "gainful protein review",
      "is gainful third party tested",
      "gainful protein powder reviews",
      "is gainful protein healthy",
      "gainful lead prop 65"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clean Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clean-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Gainful Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone weighing up the quiz-based subscription protein: what is in each of the seven formulas, what the brand actually says about testing, and what a scoop really costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Which Formula to Buy</a>
      <a href="#how-it-works">How the Quiz Works</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in Each Formula</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#lead-lactose">Lead, Lactose, Sweeteners</a>
      <a href="#buyer-reviews">Buyer Reviews</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Gainful vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Gainful is a clean, short-label protein with a premium price and a weaker testing story than its marketing suggests. The brand says raw ingredients and finished products are third-party tested, but it names no lab, publishes no results and carries no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport seal we could find. Below we read all seven formulas, work out the cost per 25 g of protein and sum up what buyers praise and complain about.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is Gainful Protein Powder Good? The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gainful is a good protein with an ordinary testing story and a high price. Its best formula, Isolate-Only Whey, lists two ingredients and 25 g of protein in about a 28 g scoop, which is as clean as a whey label gets. But the brand's third-party testing claim is unverifiable from the outside, the plant formulas carry a lead caveat in Gainful's own FAQ, and you pay $2.28 to $3.04 per 25 g of protein at list price.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want an unflavored, unsweetened protein with a very short ingredient list and you like changing flavors with packets. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you compete in a drug-tested sport, want published heavy-metal results, or care about cost per gram, because certified isolates from bigger brands give you more for less.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Gainful by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">7</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein formulas</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20-25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.28-$3.04</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">None</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">NSF or Informed seal found</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Gainful Protein Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are four of the seven Gainful protein formulas sold on gainful.com at the time of writing, chosen because they cover the decisions most buyers face: isolate or blend, whey or plant, lean or standard. Every Gainful protein ships unflavored and unsweetened; flavor comes from separate packets. Protein and calorie figures are the brand's own per-serving claims, and prices are the 14-serving list prices on gainful.com on 21 November 2026. We could not confirm a current Gainful listing on Amazon, so the buy buttons fall back to a search; Target and Dick's Sporting Goods also list some Gainful tubs.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Does Gainful's Personalized Protein Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gainful's personalization is a quiz that matches you to one of seven fixed protein formulas, not a powder mixed to order for each customer. You answer questions about your goals, training, diet and lactose tolerance, and the site recommends a tub. At the time of writing the seven were Isolate-Only Whey, Everyday Whey, Lean Whey, Performance Whey, Collagen Whey, Everyday Plant and Lean Plant. Every one ships unflavored and unsweetened.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because the word personalized does a lot of work in Gainful's marketing. A Gainful tub is closer to a well-chosen product from a short menu than a custom blend. One buyer on Trustpilot made exactly that point, saying the quiz led to one of a handful of pre-made blends. That is not a flaw in itself; seven sensible formulas cover most needs. It just means you can compare a Gainful tub directly with any other powder on the shelf, label against label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavor comes from separate packets. Gainful sells eight: Rich Chocolate, Madagascar Vanilla, Strawberry Cream, Sea Salt Caramel, Cookies and Cream, Cinnamon Toast, Chocolate Peanut Butter and Caffe Mocha, plus variety packs. You tear one into a shake made with the unflavored base. The packets are where the sweetener lives; Fortune's review lists stevia and monk fruit as the sweeteners in most of them, and Gainful says they contain no artificial flavors, sweeteners or dyes.
          </p>

          <p>
            The default way to buy is a subscription. The product pages offer 14, 28 or 42 servings and a Subscribe and Save option that the page says can be paused or cancelled at any time. The same pages also show a 30-day money-back guarantee. Subscriptions are the source of most complaints about Gainful, which we cover in the buyer reviews section, so read the delivery interval before you check out; the pages we read gave both three and four weeks in different places.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Gainful Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gainful says it is third-party tested, but it does not name the lab, publish lot results or hold a recognized sport certification. On its lead FAQ page the brand says it tests every lot of raw materials and finished products against federal standards, screens all raw ingredients for heavy metals before use, and works with NSF-certified manufacturing facilities that follow current good manufacturing practice (cGMP). Its testing article repeats that third-party testing is part of its quality approach without adding detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those statements are not the same as a product certification. An NSF-certified facility means the factory has been audited; it says nothing about whether a given Gainful tub was tested for banned substances. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport test the finished product, lot by lot, and list it in a public database you can search. We found no Gainful listing in either, and Fortune's 2026 review of Gainful also records it as not NSF Certified for Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer to whether Gainful is third-party tested is that the brand says so and we have no reason to think it is lying, but you cannot check it. Gainful also advertises that its Isolate-Only Whey tested up to 280 times lower in lead than competitors. The page does not say which competitors, which lab or which method, so treat that as marketing rather than evidence.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT THIS MEANS FOR YOU</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fine for everyday use, not for drug-tested athletes.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you compete under WADA, NCAA or a professional league's drug policy, choose a product listed on the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database. Our guide to the best NSF certified protein powder lists verified options. Gainful is not one of them.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For recreational lifters the gap matters less. A short ingredient list leaves fewer places for contamination to hide, and whey isolate is among the lowest-risk protein sources for heavy metals. If verified testing is your priority, our guide to the <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> is the shortlist to start from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Gainful Protein? All Seven Formulas, Label by Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Gainful protein has a short label and no sweetener in the tub. The differences are the protein source and the extras. The table below uses the brand's per-serving protein and calorie claims and the ingredient lists printed on gainful.com at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Formula</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main ingredients</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Isolate-Only Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate, sunflower lecithin</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Performance Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey concentrate and isolate, organic tapioca maltodextrin, tart cherry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Everyday Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey concentrate, isolate, micellar casein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Everyday Plant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic pea protein, organic rice protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Collagen Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">80</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate, collagen peptides, vitamin C</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lean Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey concentrate and isolate, tapioca fiber, green tea extract, enzymes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lean Plant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic pea and rice protein, fiber, green tea extract, enzymes</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Isolate-Only Whey is the standout. Two ingredients, 25 g of protein and 100 calories per the brand, and a tub of 14 servings weighs 392 g, so each scoop is about 28 g and close to 90 percent protein. That is the protein density of a premium isolate. Everyday Whey and Everyday Plant sit a step below at 23 g for 120 calories, which is still a respectable ratio.
          </p>

          <p>
            Performance Whey adds organic tapioca maltodextrin, a fast carbohydrate, and CherryPURE tart cherry powder for recovery. The extra 40 calories come from that carbohydrate. Tart cherry has some research behind it for muscle soreness, but the dose per scoop is not stated on the page, so we cannot tell whether it matches the amounts used in studies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen Whey is the one to read carefully. It lists 21 g of protein per serving, but part of that is collagen, which is an incomplete protein low in leucine, the amino acid most linked to muscle building. For joints, skin or simply a lower-calorie shake it is fine; for muscle gain, a gram of whey does more than a gram of collagen. The Lean formulas trade 5 g of protein for soluble fiber, green tea extract and digestive enzymes. The fiber helps fullness, but you can get the same effect from food.
          </p>
        </div>

