        <div id="lead-lactose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Does Gainful Contain Lead, Lactose or Artificial Sweeteners?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gainful carries a California Prop 65 lead warning on its protein pages, contains milk in every whey formula, and has no artificial sweeteners. Each of those needs a little context before it means anything for you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lead and Prop 65</h3>

          <p>
            The Prop 65 warning appears on the whey and plant product pages we read. Gainful's FAQ says its whey products test well below the Prop 65 threshold for lead, while its plant-based products test well below the FDA's interim reference level but occasionally slightly exceed the Prop 65 threshold. The brand calls the warning a matter of transparency rather than danger. That is a fair point in part, because Prop 65's limit is very strict, but it is also an admission that the plant tubs can cross it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins pick up lead and cadmium from soil, which is why pea and rice powders tend to test higher than whey. If heavy metals are your main worry, Gainful's whey isolate is the safer choice within the range. Our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> explains what the testing programs check and which brands publish their numbers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lactose and casein</h3>

          <p>
            Isolate-Only Whey is described as virtually lactose free; the product page says 99 percent lactose free and the tub image says 98 percent. Either way it is low enough for many people with mild lactose intolerance. Everyday Whey contains whey concentrate and micellar casein, so it has more lactose and is a poor choice for anyone sensitive to dairy. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> guide covers stricter options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sweeteners</h3>

          <p>
            The base powders are unsweetened. Sweetness comes only from the optional flavor packets, which reviewers report use stevia or monk fruit. If you dislike both, you can use the unflavored powder with fruit, cocoa or coffee instead, which is one genuine advantage of the Gainful system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyer-reviews" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Gainful's Taste, Mixing and Subscription?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly praise Gainful's taste and short label and mostly complain about subscriptions, shipping and price. On Trustpilot, gainful.com held a TrustScore of about 3.2 from roughly 900 reviews when we checked, with most reviews at five stars and a noticeable cluster at one star. Trustpilot also notes that few reviews were posted in the past year, so the picture is not fully current.
          </p>

          <p>
            The praise is consistent. Reviewers like that the unflavored base tastes like plain milk and that the packets let them change flavor daily. Several mention Cinnamon Toast and Caffe Mocha as favorites, and Fortune's tester picked the same two. Buyers also praise the quiz, the packaging and, in some cases, fast replies from customer service and access to a dietitian. Fortune reported no clumps after a 60-second shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaints are just as consistent. The most common is about subscriptions: buyers say they were charged again after trying to cancel, could not find the cancel option, or found that a gift had started a subscription. Others report deliveries taking more than ten business days, slow or unhelpful support, and refused refunds. A few say the cost per serving is too high, and one alleged the scoop delivered less protein than the label states, which is a single unverified claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gainful's public replies point customers to their account page, where it says subscriptions can be delayed or cancelled before the next order date. The practical lesson is simple: put the next charge date in your calendar, cancel from your account rather than by email, and keep the confirmation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Gainful vs Transparent Labs and Momentous: Which Is Better Value?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gainful loses to Transparent Labs on value and to Momentous on verification, and wins on simplicity and flavor flexibility. All three sell themselves as clean premium proteins, so the comparison is a fair one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs 100% Grass-Fed Whey Isolate gives about 28 g of protein per serving and, per our review, holds Informed Choice status, at roughly $1.95 per 25 g at list price. That is cheaper than any Gainful tub without a discount and comes with a certification you can check. It is also sweetened with stevia, so if you want a fully unsweetened powder Gainful still has the edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Momentous whey isolate is NSF Certified for Sport and, per our <a href="/momentous-protein-powder-review/">Momentous review</a>, costs about $2.85 per 25 g at list price. That is close to Gainful's 14-serving price, but with lot-tested sport certification. For an athlete the choice between those two is easy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Gainful wins is the system: an unsweetened base, a choice of seven formulas, and flavor packets you can swap. If you mix protein into coffee, oats and baking as often as you drink shakes, that flexibility has real value. If you only ever drink one chocolate shake a day, a certified flavored isolate does the same job for less. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide ranks the alternatives by cost per 25 g.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Gainful, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Gainful if a short, unsweetened label matters more to you than price, and you like the idea of one base powder with many flavors. The Isolate-Only Whey is the formula to get for most people: the most protein per scoop, the fewest ingredients, low lactose and, by Gainful's own account, the lowest lead result in its range. Wait for a discount code and it becomes a reasonable buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Gainful if you are a tested athlete, because nothing in the range carries a sport certification you can verify. Skip the plant formulas if lead is your main concern, since the brand says they can slightly exceed the Prop 65 threshold. Skip Lean and Collagen Whey if you are buying for muscle gain, because both give less muscle-building protein per dollar than the isolate. And skip the subscription if you are the kind of buyer who forgets to cancel things.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant, or who takes regular medication should check their protein target with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily shake. For a ranked list of short-label powders across brands, start with our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading "Third-Party Tested" as Certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gainful says outside labs test its ingredients and finished lots, but that is a brand statement, not a listing you can look up. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport publish searchable databases. If your sport drug-tests you, only a product on one of those lists protects you; Gainful is not on either that we could find.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking Collagen Whey for Muscle Gain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen Whey lists 21 g of protein, but part of it is collagen, which is low in leucine and incomplete as a protein. It suits joint or skin goals or a light shake. For building muscle, Isolate-Only Whey gives 25 g of complete whey protein, so each scoop does more of the work you are paying for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Plant Tub Lead Caveat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gainful's own FAQ says its plant-based proteins occasionally slightly exceed the California Prop 65 lead threshold, while its whey tests well below it. If you are buying Gainful specifically because it markets itself as clean and low in lead, the whey isolate is the formula that actually matches that claim.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting Subscription Dates</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common Gainful complaint is being charged after trying to cancel. Before your first order, note the delivery interval, set a reminder a few days before each charge, cancel from your account page rather than by email, and keep the confirmation screen. A 30-day money-back guarantee only helps if you claim it in time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Price per Tub, Not per Gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $33.99 tub sounds cheaper than a $60 tub until you divide. Gainful works out at $2.28 to $3.04 per 25 g of protein at list price, while certified isolates from larger brands often land near $2. Always divide the price by total grams of protein, and include the cost of flavor packets.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Gainful third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Gainful says it is. The brand states that it tests every lot of raw ingredients and finished products, including heavy-metal screening, and uses NSF-certified cGMP manufacturing facilities. It does not name the lab or publish results, and we found no Gainful product on the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases. Tested athletes should choose a product with one of those certifications.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Gainful protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most healthy adults, yes. The tubs are unflavored and unsweetened with very short ingredient lists; Isolate-Only Whey is just whey isolate and sunflower lecithin, with 25 g of protein and 100 calories per the brand. The caveats are the Prop 65 lead warning, which applies most to the plant formulas, and the price. It is a food, not medicine.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Gainful protein contain lead?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Like most protein powders, Gainful products contain trace lead, mainly from soil. The brand says its whey proteins test well below the strict California Prop 65 lead threshold, while its plant proteins test below the FDA interim reference level but occasionally slightly exceed Prop 65. A Prop 65 warning appears on its product pages for that reason.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Gainful lactose free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isolate-Only Whey is close. Gainful describes it as virtually lactose free, with 98 to 99 percent lactose removed depending on which part of the page you read, which many people with mild intolerance handle well. Everyday Whey contains whey concentrate and micellar casein, so it has more lactose. The plant formulas contain no dairy at all.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Gainful flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Gainful tubs are unflavored; flavor comes from eight packets. In buyer reviews, Cinnamon Toast and Caffe Mocha come up most often as favorites, and Fortune's reviewer picked the same two. Rich Chocolate and Madagascar Vanilla are the safe choices. The variety packs are the cheapest way to find out which you like before buying a full box.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the most common Gainful complaints?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  In Trustpilot reviews, the most common complaints are about subscriptions: charges after cancelling, a hard-to-find cancel option and gifts that started a subscription. Buyers also report slow shipping, unhelpful support and a high price per serving. The common praise is for taste, the short label, the quiz and the flavor packets.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Gainful worth the money?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only at a discount, for most people. At list price Gainful costs $2.28 to $3.04 per 25 g of protein, more than certified isolates such as Transparent Labs at around $1.95. With a 30 percent promotional code, Isolate-Only Whey drops below $2 per 25 g, which is fair for a two-ingredient isolate. Without one, you are mostly paying for the flavor-packet system.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
